    "q-69244": {
      "dom_id": "step-q69244",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "$\\sqrt{{4}^{3}}=$",
      "worked_solution": "The radical $\\sqrt[n]{x}$ can be written using a fractional exponent as ${x}^{1/n}.$\n\nComparing $\\sqrt[n]{x}$ with $\\sqrt[2]{{4}^{3}},$ we get $x={4}^{3}$ and $n=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[2]{{4}^{3}}={({4}^{3})}^{1/2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the power law for exponents, we have\n\n$$\n{({4}^{3})}^{1/2}={4}^{3/2}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
